# 0018. Tool approvals in the chat

- Status: Accepted (owner, 2026-11-25; revised after Codex's critique the same day)
- Amends: [0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr) (the writer's default permission mode)
- Date: 2026-11-25

Even a writable turn can't do much today: the writer runs headless (`claude -p`), so a
tool call that needs approval (an edit in `default` mode, most `Bash` commands in any
mode) is refused, because nobody can be asked. This ADR makes roundtable the one who is
asked, so the owner approves in the chat as they would in Claude Code. Together with
[0017](0017-writer-edits-from-any-message.adr) this is the owner's "full access, so we can
work properly from rt", without bypass flags.

## Technical details
- **Claude** (writer turns only): add `--permission-prompts host` (present in 2.1.332's
  help). roundtable already drives Claude over `--input-format stream-json`, so it is the
  SDK host: Claude asks on stdout with a control request (`can_use_tool`, tool name,
  input, a request id) and waits for the matching control response on stdin (allow, or
  deny with a message).
  - The help text does not define the envelopes. The first step of the implementation is
    a recorded, sanitized, bidirectional fixture for the installed version: a correlated
    allow, a deny, a request cancelled mid-wait, and the final `result` with stdin held
    open. Message shapes in the code come from that fixture, never from this ADR.
  - stdin stays open for the whole writable turn (today it is closed after the prompt);
    it closes when the `result` arrives or the turn is cancelled.
- **In the chat**: a request becomes an approval card in the chat, and the turn's line and
  the status line show `approval waiting (Ctrl-A)`:
  ```
  claude wants to run  Bash: cargo test --locked
    [y] allow once   [a] allow `Bash(cargo test:*)` for this thread   [n] deny   [e] deny with a note
  ```
  - The card answers only when it has the focus: `Ctrl-A`, in either mode
    ([0015](0015-vim-keys.adr)), focuses the oldest waiting card, visibly (bordered,
    scrolled into view). It cancels nothing, unlike `Esc` from Insert mode, which still
    cancels running replies (and so the request); the hint says `Ctrl-A`. Until then `y`, `a`, `n` keep their usual meaning, so nothing is
    granted by keys typed for something else. `Esc` leaves the card unanswered.
  - Each answer is bound to its turn and request id. An answer to a request whose turn was
    cancelled, whose process exited, or whose participant changed identity in the
    registry is dropped, and the card closes as `expired`.
  - Several waiting requests queue in order; cancelling a turn (`Esc`/`x`) denies its
    waiting ones.
- **"Allow for this thread" grants are matched by roundtable**, not passed to Claude as
  `--allowedTools`: every request still reaches roundtable, which answers a matching one
  itself, so every decision is logged. A grant is bound to (thread, participant
  identity, tool pattern); a writer or identity change makes it inactive. Grants are
  `approval` events ([0010](0010-filesystem-event-log.adr) lists the kind), so `/resume`
  restores them; a fork ([0019](0019-fork.adr)) starts with none.
- **Every decision is logged** as an `approval` event: tool, input (redacted like any
  event), decision, who decided (`you`, `rule <grant seq>`, `expired`, `cancelled`).
- **Default `permission_mode` for the writer** becomes `acceptEdits` (edits go through,
  shown as tool calls; commands ask). This amends 0004's "unset means `default`" for
  existing configurations: an unset writer mode changes behaviour on upgrade, and the
  CHANGELOG says so. `default` and `manual` remain choosable in `models.toml`; `plan`
  there would make the writer never edit.
- **Codex** (`codex exec`) has no host approvals: it runs `workspace-write` and refuses
  escalations. Opt-in, for the writer only, a separate key from the sandbox:
  `auto_review = true` in its `models.toml` entry passes `--approve-for-me`, placed before
  the subcommand (`codex exec --approve-for-me resume …`; 0.172.0 rejects it after
  `resume`). Codex's own reviewer may then approve escalations beyond the workspace
  sandbox (network, writes outside the checkout), so this widens what Codex can do; the
  registry comment says so. `permission_mode` keeps mapping to the sandbox only.
- **Never** `bypassPermissions` or `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`: the
  registry keeps refusing them (unchanged).
- Read-only turns keep `--permission-prompts none`-equivalent behaviour: plan mode and the
  read-only sandbox, nothing to approve.
- Consequences: the writer can edit and run commands with the owner approving each new
  kind of command once, as in Claude Code. Costs: a turn can sit waiting on the owner
  (the status line shows it); a second hand-parsed protocol (control messages) to keep up
  with CLI versions; and the upgrade changes the unset writer mode.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: claude CLI `--permission-prompts host` with stream-json input (2.1.332), codex `--approve-for-me` (0.172.0)
- Alternatives:
  - `bypassPermissions` / `danger-full-access` for the writer, behind an explicit opt-in
    key: no prompts at all, closest to "full access", but anything the model decides to run
    runs, outside any sandbox for Codex; refused since 0004. The owner can choose this
    instead; it would replace the card with a startup warning.
  - Claude `auto` mode (a classifier approves): already allowed in the registry, no UI,
    but denials still end the turn with nobody to ask.
  - `--permission-prompt-tool` through roundtable's MCP server: same effect, one more
    socket round trip; the stdin control channel is already there.
